1
00:00:00,320 --> 00:00:05,780
Welcome to, uh, the Deep Dive. Today, we're looking at how modern digital registries are built, because

2
00:00:06,620 --> 00:00:11,840
usually the default assumption is that government systems will inevitably become fragmented, siloed,

3
00:00:12,480 --> 00:00:14,400
and just incredibly costly to change.

4
00:00:15,180 --> 00:00:22,920
Right. And citizens end up having their data rekeyed, you know, five different times just because the systems are totally locked into one supplier, or they just can't

5
00:00:23,440 --> 00:00:24,460
talk to each other safely.

6
00:00:24,940 --> 00:00:30,860
Exactly. So in this Deep Dive, we are uncovering the architectural blueprint of secure digital services,

7
00:00:31,780 --> 00:00:38,200
basically learning how you can translate a legal act directly into strict database rules, uh, without compromising anyone's privacy.

8
00:00:38,900 --> 00:00:42,900
Yeah. And the mechanism that prevents all that duplicated effort comes down to one central hub.

9
00:00:43,140 --> 00:00:44,360
It's called the schema file.

10
00:00:45,240 --> 00:00:47,900
Okay, so if a digital system is, say, a building,

11
00:00:48,540 --> 00:00:51,280
this schema file is the master blueprint.

12
00:00:51,980 --> 00:00:56,020
What exactly goes into this single file to stop data from being constantly rekeyed?

13
00:00:56,920 --> 00:01:03,320
Well, it's dictated by the digital registry specification, and this single JSON or YAML file basically contains everything.

14
00:01:03,540 --> 00:01:05,099
I mean, it defines all the metadata.

15
00:01:05,740 --> 00:01:07,640
M- metadata like what specifically?

16
00:01:08,240 --> 00:01:13,220
Uh, things like the name, the unique short code, the owning body, and the retention policy.

17
00:01:13,840 --> 00:01:18,660
It also covers the classification and the life cycle state, you know, tracking it from draft, published, to archived.

18
00:01:19,220 --> 00:01:21,340
Okay, so it tracks the whole life cycle.

19
00:01:22,140 --> 00:01:24,140
Does it also establish the actual data fields?

20
00:01:25,060 --> 00:01:30,040
It does. It sets up fields like text, date, and lists with very precise rules.

21
00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:35,340
It dictates if a field is required, if it must be unique, or sets a minimum and maximum.

22
00:01:36,020 --> 00:01:36,600
Oh, I see.

23
00:01:37,260 --> 00:01:42,040
And what about linking different databases together, like linking a learner to a specific school?

24
00:01:42,900 --> 00:01:48,320
Yes, it creates those links too, and it includes the rules for what happens if, for example, that school is deleted.

25
00:01:48,880 --> 00:01:52,640
Plus, every time this schema file is published, a new version is created.

26
00:01:53,260 --> 00:01:53,480
Right.

27
00:01:54,260 --> 00:01:56,080
Now, with all those structural links,

28
00:01:56,880 --> 00:02:02,140
I m- it must be incredibly tempting to just key the entire register on a national identity number.

29
00:02:03,020 --> 00:02:03,940
Why is that a bad idea?

30
00:02:04,980 --> 00:02:05,080
Oh,

31
00:02:05,680 --> 00:02:08,440
you must never store the national identity number.

32
00:02:09,199 --> 00:02:09,479
Really?

33
00:02:10,240 --> 00:02:10,720
Never? Never.

34
00:02:11,400 --> 00:02:15,080
The GovStack identity specification explicitly keeps that number secret.

35
00:02:15,620 --> 00:02:22,660
Instead, an identity authority, the PNIA, issues an identifier that is made exclusively for that specific service.

36
00:02:23,500 --> 00:02:25,360
Okay, can you give an example of how that works?

37
00:02:26,140 --> 00:02:33,780
Sure. Take Progressa. In the Progressa system, the register keeps its own unique learner number right alongside that service specific identifier.

38
00:02:34,140 --> 00:02:36,160
It entirely avoids using the national ID.

39
00:02:36,660 --> 00:02:37,680
That is so smart.

40
00:02:38,600 --> 00:02:41,600
It isolates the privacy risk, but practically speaking,

41
00:02:42,340 --> 00:02:45,200
writing this incredibly complex schema file sounds

42
00:02:45,900 --> 00:02:46,960
really prone to error.

43
00:02:47,740 --> 00:02:50,980
How is the reference architecture actually put together without mistakes?

44
00:02:51,700 --> 00:02:56,300
This is the wild part. An AI assistant actually drafts the file using written inputs.

45
00:02:56,760 --> 00:03:00,980
Wait, an AI writes the database rules just from text documents?

46
00:03:01,520 --> 00:03:06,860
Yep. You give it the Education Act, the registration forms, and the list of services reading the register.

47
00:03:07,700 --> 00:03:13,340
Impressively, the AI traces every single rule it writes back to the exact line it came from.

48
00:03:13,840 --> 00:03:18,020
Oh, wow. That's powerful. But I'm assuming a human has to review that, right?

49
00:03:18,680 --> 00:03:21,980
Definitely. The human owner always makes the final decisions.

50
00:03:22,320 --> 00:03:28,580
They pick the key, and they mark the personal data fields, which, by the way, are optional in specification version three-point-oh-dot-alpha.

51
00:03:29,160 --> 00:03:32,660
So the owner corrects the draft and then officially publishes it.

52
00:03:32,980 --> 00:03:38,820
Exactly. Okay. So to make sure it actually works in the real world, there's a validation walkthrough, right?

53
00:03:39,200 --> 00:03:41,780
Four specific steps to prove it won't become siloed.

54
00:03:42,040 --> 00:03:46,240
Right. Step one, the schema is drafted, and the register is created right from it.

55
00:03:46,580 --> 00:03:47,500
Got it. Step two?

56
00:03:48,120 --> 00:03:50,280
Step two, the register is listed and read back.

57
00:03:50,460 --> 00:03:54,380
This verifies its schema, its metadata, and confirms its published state.

58
00:03:54,760 --> 00:03:57,800
Okay, so it's live and verified. What happens in step three?

59
00:03:58,300 --> 00:04:03,480
Step three tests the rules. A record missing a required field is submitted, and it's instantly refused.

60
00:04:03,980 --> 00:04:06,880
Because the AI drafted that strict rule based on the law.

61
00:04:07,140 --> 00:04:09,080
That makes perfect sense. And step four?

62
00:04:09,580 --> 00:04:14,160
Step four, a second record submitted with the exact same learner number is refused.

63
00:04:14,800 --> 00:04:16,200
Validating the uniqueness rule.

64
00:04:16,880 --> 00:04:19,680
So it all really comes down to one schema file

65
00:04:20,320 --> 00:04:25,540
drafted by AI from the law, corrected by the owner, and keyed securely on its own number.

66
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:29,060
Yep. It completely prevents all the fragmented systems we used to see.

67
00:04:29,920 --> 00:04:30,460
It's brilliant.

